-- title:
--   Theorem 6.1, first bullet — $\{|b_x\rangle\}$ is an orthonormal basis and $T$ is unitary
-- statement:
--   Let $h:\{0,1\}^n\to\{0,1\}^n$ satisfy (6.2) and let $U_{i,a}$ ($i\in[n]$, $a\in\{0,1\}$) be single-qubit unitaries. With $U(x) := \bigotimes_i U_{i,h_i(x)}$ and $|b_x\rangle := U(x)^\dagger|x\rangle$:
--
--   1. the vectors $\{|b_x\rangle\}_{x\in\{0,1\}^n}$ are orthonormal and span $\mathcal S(n)$;
--   2. the two expressions of (6.3) agree,
--   $$T := \sum_{x} |x\rangle\langle b_x| = \sum_{x}|x\rangle\langle x|U(x);$$
--   3. $T$ is unitary.
--
--   The matrix $T$ is the unitary generated by the pure PMG of Theorem 6.1; it maps the product basis $|b_x\rangle$ back to the computational basis.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: h given by a classical Boolean circuit; each U_{i,a} given by an ordinary unitary circuit → any h with (6.2) and any 2×2 unitary matrices U_{i,a}; reason: this bullet does not use the circuits; every circuit-given instance is a special case
--   - generalization: n ∈ ℕ (positive integers; the paper writes ℕ ∪ {0} when 0 is allowed, p.19) → n : ℕ, including n = 0; reason: the n = 0 instance is true (a single vertex, trivial registers); every positive n is an instance
-- source:
--   Shmueli, What Can Be Computed with Indefinite Causal Structure?, arXiv:2610.40573 (version of 2026-11-03, sha256 d3e36399…b606), Theorem 6.1, first bullet, eq. (6.3), pp.31–32 (PDF pp.33–34)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ICSCompute_IsUSOOutmap
import Definitions.Def_ICSCompute_Tmat

namespace ICSCompute

open QAlgorithms

/-- **Theorem 6.1, first bullet, with eq. (6.3)** (Shmueli, *What Can Be Computed with Indefinite
Causal Structure?*, arXiv:2610.40573, version of 2026-11-03, sha256 d3e36399…b606, Theorem 6.1,
pp.31–32 (PDF pp.33–34)).

Let `h : {0,1}ⁿ → {0,1}ⁿ` satisfy (6.2) and let `U_{i,a}` (`i ∈ [n]`, `a ∈ {0,1}`) be
single-qubit unitaries; `U(x) := ⨂ᵢ U_{i,hᵢ(x)}` and `|b_x⟩ := U(x)†|x⟩`. Then the vectors
`{|b_x⟩}` form an orthonormal basis of `S(n)`, and the matrix
`T := ∑_x |x⟩⟨b_x| = ∑_x |x⟩⟨x| U(x)` is unitary.

**Formalization Note** The paper's `h` and `U_{i,a}` are given by circuits; this bullet does not
use the circuits, so the statement is made for every `h` with (6.2) and all unitary `U_{i,a}`.
"Orthonormal basis" is orthonormality together with spanning `S(n) = ℂ^{2ⁿ}`
(`EuclideanSpace ℂ (Qubits n)`). Both expressions of (6.3) are stated: `T` is defined as
`∑_x |x⟩⟨b_x|` and shown equal to `∑_x |x⟩⟨x|U(x)`, the row vector `⟨x|U(x)` being
`vecMul (Pi.single x 1) (U(x))`. -/
theorem theorem_6_1_basis {n : ℕ} (h : Qubits n → Qubits n) (hh : Eq62 h)
    (U : Fin n → Bool → Matrix Bool Bool ℂ) (hU : ∀ i a, U i a ∈ Matrix.unitaryGroup Bool ℂ) :
    Orthonormal ℂ (fun x : Qubits n => (WithLp.toLp 2 (bvec U h x) : EuclideanSpace ℂ (Qubits n))) ∧
      Submodule.span ℂ
          (Set.range fun x : Qubits n => (WithLp.toLp 2 (bvec U h x) : EuclideanSpace ℂ (Qubits n))) =
        ⊤ ∧
      Tmat U h = ∑ x : Qubits n, Matrix.vecMulVec (Pi.single x 1) (Matrix.vecMul (Pi.single x 1) (Ux U h x)) ∧
      Tmat U h ∈ Matrix.unitaryGroup (Qubits n) ℂ := by sorry

end ICSCompute
